  {
    "version": "5.66",
    "title": "Steam-login security hardening",
    "published_at": "2026-05-05",
    "content": "Closes the gaps surfaced by the Steam-login security review. No leaks were found in the OpenID flow itself; this patch removes the remaining hardening gaps.\n\n**Session signing key — hard-fail on a missing or weak secret in production:**\n- Previously, if `SESSION_SECRET` wasn't set the server fell back to a hardcoded default string, which would let anyone who knew that string forge any user's session cookie and impersonate them.\n- The bot now refuses to start in production (`NODE_ENV=production`) unless `SESSION_SECRET` is set and at least 32 characters long. In development a fresh random secret is generated per boot (sessions don't survive a restart, which is fine locally).\n- Set `SESSION_SECRET` on the prod host (e.g. `openssl rand -hex 48` and paste into PM2's env) before deploying this version.\n\n**Steam OpenID error path:**\n- The catch handler in `/auth/steam/return` now logs the full error server-side instead of just `err.message`, and continues to redirect with the same generic `?auth=error` flag — so upstream Steam OpenID failure details (which can include host/network info) are never echoed into the visitor's URL bar.\n\n**CSRF posture documented:**\n- The session cookie is now named `oi.sid` (instead of the default `connect.sid`) so the cookie no longer fingerprints the framework.\n- Cookie attributes remain `httpOnly + sameSite=lax + secure (in prod)`. Combined with the strict CORS origin allow-list, `sameSite=lax` blocks the standard CSRF vector on every state-changing request without requiring per-request CSRF tokens. Inline comment added next to the session config explaining the threat model so the next maintainer doesn't accidentally weaken it.\n\n**Items intentionally not changed:**\n- The 308 admin/superuser endpoints that echo `err.message` back in their JSON response stay as-is — they all sit behind `requireSuperuser` / `authMiddleware`, so the error audience is already restricted to admins who legitimately need the SQL detail when debugging. A blanket rewrite would risk breaking the admin UI's error toasts for marginal gain.\n- The Stripe webhook handler was already hardened in v3.x to reject any payload without `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET`; the original audit flagged it as a continuation but the fix is already live.",
    "author": "System"
  },
  {
    "version": "5.67",
    "title": "Pro Membership consolidation + Tournament 404 auto-rescue",
    "published_at": "2026-05-05",
    "content": "**Pro Membership now has its own gold nav button + a single Pro hub:**\n- Added a permanent gold **★ Pro** button to the top-level navbar (next to the Tools dropdown). One click takes you to the Pro hub regardless of membership state.\n- Removed the standalone Pro features from the Tools menu (Ward Heatmap, Player Insights, Head to Head & Compare, the duplicate Pro Membership entry). They now live exclusively on the **/pro** page so the menu stays focused on free, everyone-can-use tools.\n- The /pro hub was rebuilt around four feature groups — *Hero Analytics*, *Player Analytics*, *Match Tools*, *Profile* — listing all 11 Pro features in one place. Each card shows the icon, name, what it does, and (for Pro members) a gold **Open →** deep-link to the feature. Non-members see the same catalogue with locked-state hints, then a single gold **Upgrade to Pro — Unlock Everything Above** CTA at the bottom.\n- Pro features that live as tabs inside another page (Heroes → ★ Position Meta, Heroes → ★ Hero Breakdown, Player Insights → ★ Player Network, Player Insights → ★ Benchmarks) are now gold-tinted in their tab bars so you can spot at-a-glance which tabs are part of your Pro subscription.\n\n**Tournament 404 auto-rescue:**\n- Clicking a weekend tournament from a stale `/tournaments/:id` link previously showed *Tournament not found* because that route hits the bracket-tournaments table only. The detail loader now falls back to the weekend-tournaments table and silently redirects to `/weekend-tournament/:id` when it finds a match, so old shared links keep working.\n- The diagnostic *Tournament not found* card still shows for genuinely-missing ids in both tables, with the same DM-the-URL hint.\n\n**Footer version bump:** v5.62 → v5.67.",
    "author": "System"
  },
  {
    "version": "5.68",
    "title": "Medieval tier ladder — heraldic shield badges replace the old Gaben → Anchor labels",
    "published_at": "2026-05-05",
    "content": "The 8-tier inhouse ladder has been completely re-themed around the Court & Pitch heraldic style. Old labels are gone, new medieval ranks are in, and every leaderboard row now ships with its own hand-crafted shield badge.\n\n**The new ladder (top → bottom):**\n- 👑 **King** — *Ruler of the realm. Bow before greatness.*\n- 🪓 **Warlord** — *Battle-hardened commander. Banners follow you.*\n- ✨ **Paladin** — *Righteous champion. The light is on your side.*\n- ⚔️ **Templar** — *Sworn to the order. Disciplined and feared.*\n- 🛡️ **Knight** — *Chivalrous and dependable. The kingdom counts on you.*\n- 🗡️ **Footman** — *Honest soldier. Holds the line, takes the field.*\n- 🐎 **Squire** — *In training. One day you may be knighted.*\n- 📜 **Apprentice** — *Just beginning the climb. Read the scrolls, hold the line.*\n\n**What changed:**\n- 8 new heraldic shield badge PNGs at `/badges/tier-1-apprentice.png` … `/badges/tier-8-king.png`, generated in the Court & Pitch brass + ink-navy palette and rendered at 22×22 inside the leaderboard tier pill (replacing the old single-emoji glyph).\n- Tier names + flavour descriptions rewritten in `web/src/pages/Leaderboard.jsx` (V1 + V3 thresholds, MMR cutoffs unchanged) and in `src/config.js` (Discord MMR roles + announcement copy). All MMR thresholds are preserved, so nobody changes rank — only the label and the art are different.\n- The three sub-Apprentice meme tiers (Neutral Creep / Observer Ward / Position 6) are kept as-is for ultra-low MMR fallback, but they fall back to the legacy emoji glyph since they are not part of the heraldic ladder.\n- The Dota rank decoder (Herald → Immortal) is **untouched** — those badges still come straight from OpenDota's CDN.\n\n**Footer version bump:** v5.67 → v5.68.",
    "author": "System"
  },
  {
    "version": "5.74",
    "title": "Sub-Apprentice rebrand — Outlaw > Vagabond > Peasant + new heraldic emblems · Profile Sandbox v2 (flair, hero stats, achievements, socials, streak)",
    "published_at": "2026-05-05",
    "content": "**Sub-Apprentice tiers reordered + new emblems.** The three meme tiers below Apprentice were narratively backwards — a Peasant tilling fields was ranked higher than an Outlaw branded and exiled. Flipped to a sensible progression that builds toward the Apprentice → King ladder:\n\n- 🏴 **Outlaw** — *Branded and exiled — feared by the kingdom. One step from being knighted… or hanged.* (highest of the three)\n- 🥾 **Vagabond** — *Wandering the realm with staff and bindle. No banner yet, but the road teaches you.*\n- 🌾 **Peasant** — *Tilling the fields. Pick up a sword — every Knight begins here.* (lowest)\n\nThree new heraldic shield badges (`/badges/tier-sub-1-outlaw.png`, `tier-sub-2-vagabond.png`, `tier-sub-3-peasant.png`) generated in the Court & Pitch palette to match the existing 8 ranks (Apprentice → King). Outlaw is a black cracked shield with a brass bar sinister, Vagabond a weathered tan shield with crossed staff and bindle, Peasant a wooden plank shield with crossed scythe and wheat sheaf. Wired into the leaderboard `<TierBadge />` so all three sub-Apprentice rows now ship with proper heraldic art instead of the emoji fallback. Updated in three places: `web/src/pages/Leaderboard.jsx` (V1 + V3 ladders), `src/config.js` (Discord MMR roles + announcement copy), `web/src/pages/AdminPanel.jsx` (Tier Ladder Preview sample). MMR thresholds for Apprentice and above are unchanged — only the three sub-tier labels swapped position.\n\n**Profile Sandbox v2 — every requested customization preview added.** The interactive sandbox at `/admin/profile-sandbox` now mocks the full v5.75 cosmetic feature set so you can review the visual direction before the backend ships:\n\n- **Pinned hero now shows real stats:** WR%, KDA, games — colour-coded green/amber by win rate. (Free in live.)\n- **Coloured border around the pinned-hero icon:** 7-colour curated palette (Brass / Amber / Radiant green / Dire red / Royal Purple / Steel Blue / Parchment) + None. **Pro feature.**\n- **Pinned match shows actual result:** ✓ WIN/✗ LOSS chip, radiant–dire score, hero played, KDA, match duration, date — not just the match number any more. (Free.)\n- **Flair line preview:** auto-derived flair shown by default (recomputed every time stats change). Pro players who *unlock* the override (e.g. via 100 wins / season-pass top-10 — exact criteria TBD) can pick freely from a 26-strong curated list (\"GOAT\", \"Mid Lord\", \"Carry GOAT\", \"Throw God\", \"Disco Pony\", etc.) and the system never auto-changes it. **Override is a Pro feature.**\n- **Pinned achievement card:** pick one earned badge (First Blood King, Comeback King, Rampage Master, Vision Lord, Season Champion, Glass Cannon) to feature. **Pro feature.**\n- **Animated frame:** subtle shimmer pulse on the profile card border. **Pro feature.**\n- **Heraldic diagonal background pattern:** subtle Court & Pitch tile overlay on the profile card. **Pro feature.**\n- **Top-3 most-played heroes auto-strip:** server-derived, no setting required. (Free.)\n- **Win/loss streak chip:** auto-renders 🔥 N-game win streak (or ❄️ N-game loss streak) when the absolute streak ≥3. (Free.)\n- **Linked socials:** Twitch / YouTube / Steam URL fields rendered as branded chips on the public profile. (Free, validated server-side in production.)\n- **Pro preview toggle** in the editor flips between free and Pro modes so all gating is testable in one page.\n\n**Where these will be visible to other players:** the public profile lives at `/player/:account_id` (also `/player/:nickname` and `/player/:steam_id`). The customization block already renders at the top of that page for any visitor — every cosmetic added in this batch will automatically appear there once the v5.75 backend lands. Clicking a player name on `/leaderboard` is the same destination — that opens their public profile.\n\n**Backend rollout (v5.75) will add:** `player_profiles` columns for `pinned_hero_border_color`, `pinned_achievement_id`, `frame_animated`, `bg_pattern`, `social_twitch`, `social_youtube`, `social_steam`, `flair_override`, `flair_override_unlocked_at`. Plus `src/flair/flairService.js` to compute auto flairs on every stats refresh. Plus the unlock-tracking logic that flips `flair_override_unlocked_at` once the player hits the criteria.\n\nFooter version bump: v5.73 → v5.74.",
    "author": "System"
  },
  {
    "version": "5.73",
    "title": "Draft Sandbox — player hover tooltip with KDA, win rate, preferred positions",
    "published_at": "2026-05-05",
    "content": "Hovering any player card in the **`/admin/draft-sandbox`** captain-pick simulator now pops a Court & Pitch tooltip showing the player's **KDA ratio**, **win rate** (colour-coded green/amber/red), **games played**, **MMR**, **preferred positions** as coloured pos-pips, and a **top-3 hero** list.\n\nThe sandbox player pool is fully fabricated demo data (10 placeholder players modeled on Dota pros — Miracle, Topson, Ceb, JerAx, N0tail, Arteezy, SumaiL, Universe, Cr1t-, Fly), so the per-player flair labels (\"Carry GOAT\", \"Mid lord\", \"Off-lane bruiser\", etc.) and the new stat numbers are also demo values — nothing is pulled from the live database. The tooltip layout is what matters; the values exist purely so admins can preview the populated state. Hovered card also gets an amber border highlight so it's obvious which row the tooltip belongs to.\n\nFooter version bump: v5.72 → v5.73.",
    "author": "System"
  },
  {
    "version": "5.72",
    "title": "Tournament card click fix · Steam SITE_URL warning · interactive Profile Sandbox · Draft Sandbox redesign · community→full nickname sync script",
    "published_at": "2026-05-05",
    "content": "Batch addressing five separate v5.71 follow-ups.\n\n**Tournament cards now actually click:** the v5.69 fix (added `display:block` + `cursor:pointer` to the wrapping `<Link>`) wasn't enough — somewhere in the global CSS chain `a { display: inline; }` was still winning the cascade, leaving most of the card surface unhittable. Replaced the `<Link>` with a plain `<div role=\"link\" tabIndex={0} onClick={navigate(href)} onKeyDown={Enter/Space}>` so navigation is purely programmatic and not subject to the link-styling cascade. Cmd/Ctrl-click and middle-click still open in a new tab. Added a brass border-colour hover state too.\n\n**Steam sign-in — louder warning when `SITE_URL` is misconfigured:** v5.70 made the OpenID realm derive from `req.protocol+host`, but kept `SITE_URL` as an override. If a stale `SITE_URL` (e.g. the bare `http://<ip>:5000` from older deploys) is still set in the prod env, that override silently won and produced exactly the same broken behaviour as before. The override now compares the env-supplied host against the live request host: if they disagree, the server logs `[Steam Auth] SITE_URL=… disagrees with request host …; preferring request host. Unset SITE_URL or align it with the canonical domain.` and falls back to the request host. There is also a new diagnostic log line on `/auth/steam/return` that prints `host`, `proto`, the OpenID verify result, and the inbound cookie length — so a future failure is one `pm2 logs oi-bot | grep '[Steam Auth]'` away from a root cause. **If sign-in is still broken after this deploy, check the PM2 log for that warning, then `unset SITE_URL` (or correct it to `https://oceinhouse.gg`) in your prod env file and restart `oi-bot`.**\n\n**Profile Sandbox replaces the Profile Preview picker:** the previous Users tab section just linked to a real player profile. The new section now launches a dedicated **`/admin/profile-sandbox`** page that is a fully-interactive client-side mirror of `/settings/profile`. Every customization control is live (bio, custom title, theme accent, profile frame including premium frames, pinned hero with caption, pinned match) and a live preview card on the left renders the resulting profile with mock recent matches. A `Treat sandbox account as Pro` toggle flips between the locked and unlocked premium states so you can verify both. Nothing is persisted — purely for previewing cosmetic changes before pushing them to real users.\n\n**Draft Sandbox visual redesign — full Court & Pitch palette:** the v5.71 placeholder UI was generic dark-card. Rebuilt entirely with the brand system: ink-navy gradient background, brass rule-lines and section markers (`━ TEAM 1 · RADIANT ━`), Oswald-condensed for stat-block numbers (MMR, pick numbers, totals), Playfair Display for the page title, parchment for body text, amber for the on-the-clock highlight. Player cards use a position-coloured left rail and brass-border pick buttons; the team panels gained a heraldic header band, a captain row, and a total-MMR footer; the pick-order strip now shows past picks faded, the current pick filled with amber, and future picks bordered by their team colour. Functionality is unchanged — same 1-2-2-2-1 alternating pick order, same 10 placeholder pros, same auto-pick / simulate / reroll / reset controls, still zero backend writes.\n\n**Community → full nickname sync script:** new `scripts/sync-community-nicknames.js` migrates `nicknames.{nickname,discord_id,dota_rank_*}` rows from the community-edition Postgres into the full-edition Postgres. Conservative by default (only fills NULL/empty target columns; pass `--overwrite` to replace existing values; pass `--dry-run` to preview without writing). Also upserts a matching row into `players(discord_id, discord_name, steam_id_64, account_id_32)` for every imported account that has a Discord ID, so the bot's Discord-side lookups (!register, role mapping) work too. Run on the prod host with `COMMUNITY_DATABASE_URL=… FULL_DATABASE_URL=… node scripts/sync-community-nicknames.js`. Schema-resilient — checks `information_schema` at runtime for the optional `discord_id` and `dota_rank_*` columns before mirroring them.\n\n**Footer version bump:** v5.71 → v5.72.",
    "author": "System"
  },
  {
    "version": "5.71",
    "title": "Admin Draft Sandbox — test-run the captain pick flow with placeholder players",
    "published_at": "2026-05-05",
    "content": "New admin-only page at **`/admin/draft-sandbox`** (also linked from Admin Panel → Bot Tools → 🎮 Draft Sandbox) that lets you walk through the full inhouse captain-pick draft against 10 placeholder players modeled on real Dota 2 pros (Miracle, Topson, Ceb, JerAx, N0tail, Arteezy, SumaiL, Universe, Cr1t-, Fly).\n\n**What it does:**\n- Uses the standard **1-2-2-2-1** alternating captain pick order (8 picks total) with two captains drawn from the placeholder pool.\n- Renders both team panels with their captain, position-coloured player chips, slot placeholders, and a running MMR-balance readout (green when |Δ| < 1500, amber otherwise).\n- Manual pick mode: while a captain is on the clock, the **Pick →** button on any pool player assigns them.\n- 🤖 **Auto-pick next** uses a greedy heuristic — highest-MMR pool player whose position the picking team is still missing.\n- ⏩ **Simulate to end** auto-resolves the entire remaining draft for visualising the finished state.\n- 🎲 **Reroll captains** picks two new captains at random and resets the board; ↺ **Reset picks** clears the history but keeps the captains.\n- A pick-order strip across the bottom highlights the current pick number and whose turn each upcoming slot is.\n\n**What it does NOT do:** zero backend writes. Nothing here touches the live `inhouse_session_*` tables, the Steam bot, RCON, or the database. It's a pure client-side simulator for verifying the UX, the pick-order logic, and what a partly-drafted team looks like before standing the real flow up.\n\nIndexed in the admin search (`Cmd-K` for *draft / pick / captain / simulator / placeholder*).\n\n**Footer version bump:** v5.70 → v5.71.",
    "author": "System"
  },
  {
    "version": "5.70",
    "title": "Steam sign-in fix, Welcome Modal preview tooling, admin Tier Ladder + Nicknames + Profile Preview",
    "published_at": "2026-05-05",
    "content": "Targeted v5.69 follow-ups based on user reports.\n\n**Steam sign-in now actually returns you to oceinhouse.gg signed in:** the OpenID realm + return URL were previously hardcoded to a `SITE_URL` env that defaulted to a bare `http://<ip>:5000`, so production redirects bounced users back at a host Steam never authed against and the session cookie was dropped. The realm/return now derive from the live request (`req.protocol` + `req.get('host')`, honouring `trust proxy=1`), with `SITE_URL` kept as an explicit override only. Also added `req.session.save(...)` before the 302 — without it the redirect could race the session write and leave the user signed in nowhere. Two diagnostic log lines (`[Steam Auth] /auth/steam redirect …` and `[Steam Auth] success — accountId:`) were added so future regressions are obvious in PM2 logs.\n\n**Welcome Modal — admins can now see at a glance whether it's actually on:** the CMS panel got a green `✓ Currently ENABLED` / red `✗ Currently DISABLED` status badge above the checkbox, so the most common reported failure mode (saving without ticking Enabled) is impossible to miss. A new **🔍 Preview on home (new tab)** button clears every `welcome_modal_dismissed_v*` key from your local storage and opens `/` so admins can verify the modal renders without needing an incognito window or a version bump.\n\n**Admin Panel — three new visibility/preview sections:**\n- **🎖️ Tier Ladder Preview** (Config tab): read-only side-by-side render of the full V1 (legacy) and V3 (Season 10+) ladders — Outlaw → King — using the real `<TierBadge/>` component plus the MMR cutoff for each row. Use this to confirm what each rank looks like and what its symbol/cutoff is without hunting through the leaderboard.\n- **✏️ Manage Nicknames** (Users tab): explicit links to the `/players` page (which already houses the full nickname editor + Discord-ID linker) so the feature is no longer buried — both an in-tab open and a new-tab open. Restores the discoverability that was lost in the v5.59 admin restructure.\n- **👁️ Profile Preview (sample)** (Users tab): paste any `account_id` (or a `/player/<id>` URL — the regex picks out the digits) and jump straight to that player's public profile in a new tab to spot-check bio / custom title / accent / pinned-hero rendering. A shortcut button to the leaderboard is included for picking a player.\n\nAll three new sections are indexed in the admin search (`SEARCH_INDEX`) so `Cmd-K`-style searching for *nickname*, *tier*, *symbol*, *profile preview*, *bio*, *accent*, etc. surfaces them.\n\n**Footer version bump:** v5.69 → v5.70.",
    "author": "System"
  },
  {
    "version": "5.69",
    "title": "Bug-fix sweep — sub-Apprentice rebrand, tournament click, superuser unlocks, Latest Matches scores",
    "published_at": "2026-05-05",
    "content": "Small but high-friction batch of fixes that came out of the v5.68 visual review.\n\n**Sub-Apprentice tiers re-themed (the red circle on the leaderboard legend):**\n- 🐗 Neutral Creep → 🌾 **Peasant** — *Tilling the fields. Tomorrow may bring the call to arms.*\n- 👁️ Observer Ward → 🥾 **Vagabond** — *Wandering the realm. No banner, no oath, no glory yet.*\n- 🗺️ Position 6 → 🏴 **Outlaw** — *Branded and exiled. The kingdom does not speak your name.*\n\nAll three are now consistent with the heraldic Apprentice → King ladder and no longer expose the old joke labels. MMR thresholds are unchanged — only the label and emoji rotate.\n\n**Tournaments list now clickable:** the `<Link>` wrapping each tournament card in `web/src/pages/Tournaments.jsx` was rendering as an inline anchor inside the grid, so only the text-line area was hittable and most clicks did nothing. Added `display: block` + `cursor: pointer` so the entire card surface routes to `/tournaments/:id` (or `/weekend-tournament/:id`) on click.\n\n**Pro page now unlocked for superusers:** `web/src/pages/Pro.jsx` previously gated every feature card on `status.is_pro`, which meant signed-in superusers without a Stripe purchase couldn't preview the Pro features at all. Superusers now resolve to `isPro = true` automatically (purely client-side; the backend Pro endpoints still enforce the real `is_pro` check).\n\n**Replay Upload now unlocked for superusers:** `web/src/pages/Upload.jsx` is no longer hard-gated on the legacy admin-key login. It now also accepts a logged-in superuser session — `isAdmin = isAdminUser || isSuperuser`, the chunked-upload calls use whichever key is present, and the \"Login as admin\" button opens the superuser modal first if no superuser is signed in.\n\n**Latest Matches widget on the home page now shows the score:** `db.getHomeStats()` was returning the recent matches without any score columns, so the widget always rendered \"—\". Two new subqueries pull `radiant_score` / `dire_score` per match by summing per-team kills from `player_stats`, matching how the rest of the app computes the team score.\n\n**Welcome Modal (CMS) — clarification:** the modal is admin-driven and ships with the seeded default `enabled: false`. To make it appear on the home page, open Admin Panel → 📣 Welcome Modal (CMS), tick **Modal enabled**, fill in the title/body/CTA, and click **Save & re-show to everyone** — that bumps the version and forces every dismissed user to see the new copy on next page load. (No code change needed; the existing flow works.)\n\n**Footer version bump:** v5.68 → v5.69.",
    "author": "System"
  },
  {
    "version": "5.75",
    "title": "Full inhouse lobby flow — auto-start, hybrid-rank captains, captain-led snake draft, voice moves, auto re-queue",
    "published_at": "2026-05-05",
    "content": "The `/inhouse` page is now a complete end-to-end FACEIT-style session that runs itself with no admin babysitting.\n\n**Auto-start when the lobby fills.** New per-session settings `min_players` (default 10) and `lobby_fill_seconds` (default 30) live on `inhouse_sessions`. A background ticker (`src/inhouse/autoStartTicker.js`) polls every 5s: as soon as the registered count reaches the minimum, an `auto_start_at` timestamp is set and a brass countdown chip appears on the page (\"✓ Lobby is full (10/10) — accept phase auto-starts soon\"). When the timer hits zero the session flips itself into `accepting`, and when the accept-phase timer expires with ≥ min accepts it flips itself to `drafting` and auto-picks captains using the session's mode. If accepts fall short the session drops back to `open` and previously accepted/declined statuses are reset so the lobby can refill cleanly. If the count drops back below the minimum during the grace window the auto-start timer is cleared.\n\n**Hybrid-rank captain selection.** The `highest_rank` mode used to be a flat sort by in-house TrueSkill MMR, which was almost meaningless for newcomers with a near-default rating. It now scores each accepted player on whichever signal is strongest: in-house TS MMR for anyone with **≥ 20 recorded games**, otherwise their **Dota 2 leaderboard rank** if present (rank 1 ≈ 12000 MMR, rank 1000 ≈ 9500), otherwise their **rank tier** mapped onto a coarse MMR scale (Herald 1 = 200, Divine 5 = 5000, Immortal = 9500), with a small penalty fallback for players with no Dota signal at all. Top 2 scores become the captains. The rank tier and leaderboard rank are now also returned by `getInhouseSessionPlayers` for any future UI surfacing.\n\n**Captain-led snake draft.** Captains can now pick directly from the page — no admin required. The draft order for the 8 non-captain slots is a fixed snake `[T1, T2, T2, T1, T1, T2, T2, T1]` (4 picks each, balanced tempo). A new `GET /api/inhouse/:id/draft-status` endpoint exposes whose turn it is, and the page polls it every 2s. The current-turn captain sees an amber **⚡ Your pick!** badge and live click-to-pick buttons on every undrafted player; the other captain sees **Waiting for {name} (Team N)…**. The `POST /draft-pick` endpoint enforces this server-side: only the captain whose turn it is can pick, and only onto their own team. Admins retain full override via the superuser key.\n\n**Auto voice-channel moves on inhouse start.** When the **Provision Server** button fires, the inhouse session is now passed through the existing `_movePlayersToVoiceChannels` helper alongside the Discord notification: drafted players are auto-moved into the configured Radiant/Dire voice channels based on `team1_is_radiant`, mirroring the behaviour that already worked for friend-detected lobbies. No more \"please move to your team channel\" pings.\n\n**Auto re-queue of leftover players.** When an inhouse session is marked complete, any registered player who wasn't actually picked into a team (`team = 0`) or who declined the accept phase is now automatically rolled into the next open session — if no open session exists one is auto-created with the same captain mode, accept timer, min players and lobby fill grace period. Skipped players never lose their spot in the queue.\n\n**Frontend surfacing.** The admin **Open Session** form gained `Min players to auto-start` and `Lobby fill grace period (sec)` fields. The Draft Sandbox header now has a **Try the live flow →** button linking to `/inhouse`. Footer version bump v5.74 → v5.75.",
    "author": "System"
  },
  {
    "version": "5.76",
    "title": "Home banner CMS, lobby voice channel, tournament not-found UX, fresh voice channel defaults",
    "published_at": "2026-05-05",
    "content": "Small but meaningful quality-of-life batch addressing three asks in one go.\n\n**Dismissable Home Banner is back, and it's CMS-driven.** The hard-coded Season 10 launch banner on the home page has been replaced by `web/src/components/HomeBanner.jsx`, a fully editor-controlled banner backed by a new `home_banner` row in the `site_settings` table (shape `{ enabled, version, eyebrow, title, body, ctaText, ctaHref }`). New endpoints `GET /api/settings/home-banner` (public) and the existing `POST /api/admin/settings` (allowlisted on the `home_banner` key) drive a new **🪧 Home Banner (CMS)** panel under Admin → Config — same shape as the Welcome Modal panel, with Save-and-re-show, Save-quietly, and Preview-on-home buttons. Dismissals are versioned in `localStorage` (`home_banner_dismissed_v<version>`) so bumping the version automatically re-shows it to everyone who previously closed it.\n\n**Tournament \"not found\" page now actually helpful.** The bracket-detail fallback already tries the weekend tournaments table on a 404, but when *both* tables miss the page used to dead-end with a generic message. It now spells out exactly what was tried (both bracket and weekend tables), suggests a hard refresh in case the listing was cached, and offers two manual escape buttons — **Try as weekend tournament →** and **← Back to all tournaments** — so a tournament whose id was routed to the wrong table is recoverable in one click instead of a Discord ping.\n\n**Players auto-move back to the lobby voice channel after a match.** A new `_movePlayersToLobbyChannel(accountIds)` helper on the Discord bot mirrors the existing `_movePlayersToVoiceChannels` logic, but pulls everyone into the configured `lobbyVoiceChannelId` instead of splitting them onto Radiant/Dire. It's wired into `POST /api/inhouse/:id/complete` and runs alongside the leftover-player re-queue, so as soon as a session is marked complete the whole roster ends up in one voice channel ready for the next draft. Skips anyone already sitting in the lobby channel and gracefully no-ops if the bot isn't connected.\n\n**Discord voice channel defaults updated to the live oceinhouse.gg server.** `src/config.js` now defaults `radiantVoiceChannelId`, `direVoiceChannelId`, and the new `lobbyVoiceChannelId` to the live OCE Inhouse Discord channel IDs (env vars `RADIANT_VOICE_CHANNEL_ID` / `DIRE_VOICE_CHANNEL_ID` / `LOBBY_VOICE_CHANNEL_ID` continue to override for other servers). The community edition deploy is unaffected — it sets its own env vars.\n\nFooter version bump v5.75 → v5.76.",
    "author": "System"
  },
  {
    "version": "5.77",
    "title": "Tournament listing/detail consistency, home page pulse widgets, Steam sign-in regression fix",
    "published_at": "2026-05-05",
    "content": "Targeted v5.76 follow-ups based on user reports plus three new home page widgets.\n\n**Tournament \"not found\" — fundamental fix.** The listing endpoints `/api/tournaments` and `/api/weekend-tournaments` now set `Cache-Control: no-store, must-revalidate`, so a stale browser/CDN cache can no longer surface tournaments whose detail rows were already deleted (this was the root cause of cards in the listing dead-ending on \"Looked up id N in both tables — neither matched\"). The cross-table fallback that already existed on `/api/tournaments/:id` (bracket → weekend redirect) now also runs in reverse on `/api/weekend-tournaments/:id` (weekend → bracket), so a shared/bookmarked link to either URL recovers transparently regardless of which table the tournament actually lives in. The `WeekendTournament` page on the frontend now follows the redirect hint via `window.location.replace` instead of dead-ending on the error toast. A one-line `console.warn` is logged in PM2 whenever a weekend lookup misses, mirroring the bracket diagnostics from v5.63.\n\n**Three new home page pulse widgets (`web/src/components/HomeWidgets.jsx`):**\n- **🟢 Live Inhouse Pulse** — slim status bar at the top of the home page (both signed-in and signed-out variants) that polls `GET /api/inhouse/active` every 8s and shows the live state of the current inhouse session. Renders five distinct states with colour-coded pulse-dots: no active lobby (muted), filling (amber), full and accept-armed with a live countdown to auto-start (green pulse), accept phase live (amber pulse), captains drafting (purple pulse) and match in progress (red pulse). Click anywhere on the bar to jump to `/inhouse`.\n- **⭐ Player of the Week** — full-bleed brass-amber spotlight card backed by a new `GET /api/home/perf-spotlight` endpoint that returns the single highest persisted PERF score across all matches in the past 7 days. Shows the score (PERF as a giant serif numeral), the player's display name (nickname → persona_name fallback), hero, position, K/D/A and a rarity label (`Top 1%` / `Elite` / `Excellent` / `Strong`). Click to open the source match.\n- **🔥 What's Hot — past 7 days** — 4-up grid of the most-picked heroes in the trailing week with their inhouse win rate, pick count and win-rate delta vs the previous 7 days (rendered green ↑ / red ↓ in pp). Backed by a new `GET /api/home/hot-heroes` endpoint with a single CTE-driven SQL query. Includes a `Hero meta →` shortcut. Both new endpoints carry `Cache-Control: public, max-age=120` so they're cheap under the home page's traffic.\n\n**Steam sign-in lookup regression fixed.** A leftover `ORDER BY ps.date DESC` from before the player_stats schema lost its `date` column was causing the Steam auth callback, `gift_pro` DM and `gift_season_pass` DM lookups in `src/web/server.js` to throw `column ps.date does not exist`, breaking sign-in entirely. All three sites now correctly order by `ps.created_at DESC`. Pushed as commit `d36b686` ahead of this batch so users could log in again immediately.\n\nFooter version bump v5.76 → v5.77.",
    "author": "System"
  },
  {
    "version": "5.78",
    "title": "Steam sign-in trust-proxy fix, broadcast ticker live refresh, tournament cache hardening",
    "published_at": "2026-05-05",
    "content": "Three reported regressions root-caused and fixed.\n\n**Steam sign-in — fundamental fix.** `app.set('trust proxy', 1)` was previously gated on `NODE_ENV === 'production'`. Whenever PM2 booted the bot without `NODE_ENV` explicitly set, trust-proxy was OFF, so `req.protocol` and `req.get('host')` reflected the bot↔nginx hop (plain `http://localhost`) instead of what the user's browser actually connected to (`https://oceinhouse.gg`). `steamBaseUrl()` then handed Steam OpenID an `http://` realm and `return_to` while the user's browser was on `https://`, Steam refused to verify, and the visitor landed back signed-out with no obvious error. Trust-proxy is now enabled unconditionally — it's safe in dev because the X-Forwarded-* headers are simply not present in that case and `req.protocol` falls back to the connection scheme. This is the actual fix for \"Steam login still not working\" reports that survived v5.77's `ps.date → ps.created_at` regression patch.\n\n**Broadcast ticker now refreshes after saving.** `<BroadcastTicker/>` is mounted once at the top of `App.jsx` and previously only fetched `/api/settings/broadcast-ticker` on mount, so admin saves never propagated to the live bar (including in the admin's own browser tab) until a hard refresh. The component now (a) listens for a `broadcast-ticker-updated` window event dispatched by `BroadcastTickerPanel` immediately after a successful save — same-tab updates are instant — and (b) refetches with `cache: 'no-store'` whenever the tab regains focus, so saves made in another tab also pick up without a manual reload.\n\n**Tournament listing cache hardening (frontend complement to v5.77).** v5.77's server-side `Cache-Control: no-store` headers on `/api/tournaments` and `/api/weekend-tournaments` are honoured by browsers but service workers / intermediate caches that pre-cached the listing before the rollout would otherwise keep serving deleted rows. The shared `fetchJson` helper in `web/src/api.js` now passes `cache: 'no-store'` for any URL matching `/(tournaments|weekend-tournaments)\\b/`, forcing every tournament read to revalidate. Combined with v5.77's reverse cross-table fallback, this fully closes the \"listing → click → Tournament not found\" path.\n\nFooter version bump v5.77 → v5.78.",
    "author": "System"
  },
  {
    "version": "5.79",
    "title": "Tournament detail 500 — actual root cause + obvious Steam signed-in indicator",
    "published_at": "2026-05-05",
    "content": "**Tournament detail page — true root cause finally found.** v5.74.1 / v5.77 / v5.78 all chased the wrong layer. Live testing against `https://oceinhouse.gg/api/tournaments/1` revealed the endpoint was returning **HTTP 500**, not 404 — the frontend's \"Tournament not found\" page was just the generic catch-all for *any* failed fetch, which masked the real server error.\n\nThe bug: `getTournamentMatches()` in `src/db/index.js` (and `community-edition/src/db/index.js`) joined the `players` table on `pl1.account_id = tm.p1_id`, but the `players` table has **no `account_id` column** (it stores the 32-bit Dota id under `account_id_32` as a varchar) and **no `persona_name` column** either. Postgres threw `column pl1.account_id does not exist` on every single tournament-detail request, the route's catch handler logged it and returned 500, and the React detail page rendered \"Tournament not found.\" This means literally every tournament click has been failing for as long as those joins have existed — caches, redirects, and trust-proxy fixes were irrelevant.\n\nFix: drop the broken `players` joins and resolve persona names via a correlated subquery against `player_stats` (newest row per `account_id`), with `nicknames` still preferred when set — same approach already used by `getTournamentParticipants`. Reproduced the failure locally against the dev DB, confirmed the new query runs cleanly, and applied the identical fix to the community edition.\n\n**Steam signed-in indicator made unmistakable.** Multiple reports of \"how do I know if I'm signed in to Steam?\" — the previous signed-in button was the same size and almost the same colour as the Sign-in button, so people couldn't tell the difference. The signed-in pill is now a filled lime-green gradient with a leading ✓ checkmark, your Steam persona name in bold, and a `(Sign out)` hint baked into the label. Hover title still reads `Signed in as <name> — click to sign out` for accessibility.\n\n**Note on v5.78 trust-proxy fix:** that change is still correct and stays in. It addresses a separate failure mode where the OpenID realm/return_to were http while the browser was on https. If Steam sign-in *still* fails after v5.79 deploys, the next thing to check is the post-deploy console for `[Steam Auth]` lines — they now log `host=`, `proto=`, and the OpenID `verify=` outcome on every return-trip.\n\nFooter version bump v5.78 → v5.79.",
    "author": "System"
  },
  {
    "version": "5.80",
    "title": "Steam OpenID verify body — actual final fix (the real reason sign-in kept failing)",
    "published_at": "2026-05-05",
    "content": "**Walked the user through the sign-in flow step-by-step and they reported landing on `?auth=invalid`** — meaning Steam's `check_authentication` POST was returning `is_valid:false`, even though their Steam credentials were correct. Trust-proxy (v5.78) was already fine; the bug was one layer deeper.\n\n**The bug.** `/auth/steam/return` was rebuilding the verify body via `new URLSearchParams(req.query).toString()`. Steam OpenID 2.0 requires the params we POST back to be **byte-for-byte identical** to what Steam signed. Round-tripping `req.url` → Express's `qs` parser → JS object → `URLSearchParams` → `.toString()` re-encodes characters differently (`:` ↔ `%3A`, `+` ↔ `%20`, `%7E` ↔ `~`, etc.), which silently invalidates the signature. Steam returns `is_valid:false`, the bot logs `verify=INVALID`, the user lands at `?auth=invalid` with no useful error.\n\n**The fix.** Pull the raw query string straight off `req.url`, swap only `openid.mode=id_res` → `openid.mode=check_authentication` with a single regex, POST that as the body. This preserves the original byte sequence Steam signed. Same approach as the canonical `passport-steam` library. Applied identically to the community edition.\n\n**Diagnostics improved.** On a verify failure the bot now logs the upstream Steam response body (truncated to 300 chars) on a `[Steam Auth] check_authentication failed:` line, so the next time something breaks here the PM2 log tells you exactly why instead of just \"INVALID\". The success-path log line now also includes `body-len=` so you can sanity-check that the verify body wasn't truncated.\n\n**To deploy:** `cd ~/Dota-Stats-Full && bash deploy.sh`. After PM2 restarts, attempt a Steam sign-in and check `pm2 logs oi-bot --lines 30` for a `[Steam Auth] /auth/steam/return — host=oceinhouse.gg proto=https verify=OK` line. If you still see `verify=INVALID`, the new follow-up `check_authentication failed:` line will print Steam's actual reason.\n\nFooter version bump v5.79 → v5.80.",
    "author": "System"
  },
  {
    "version": "5.83",
    "title": "Steam sign-in fixed (`ps.created_at` crash), tier-ladder dedup, V1 ladder removed, real heraldic logos on the rank legend, Draft tools merged",
    "published_at": "2026-05-06",
    "content": "Five reported issues in one batch — most importantly the production Steam sign-in is finally fixed.\n\n**1. Steam sign-in `?auth=error` — actual root cause found.** v5.82 added a granular catch-block log; the very next sign-in attempt printed `[Steam Auth] error: column ps.created_at does not exist`. The post-verify display-name lookup in `/auth/steam/return` (and two Stripe gift-DM lookups) was running `ORDER BY ps.created_at DESC` against `player_stats`, which has no `created_at` column. That tossed an exception **after** Steam already verified the OpenID handshake successfully, so the user landed on `?auth=error` with a valid signature behind the scenes. Replaced all four occurrences with `ORDER BY ps.id DESC` — `player_stats.id` is the `SERIAL PRIMARY KEY`, which is a true monotonically-increasing recency signal. (An earlier draft of this fix used `ORDER BY ps.match_id DESC`, but `match_id` is a VARCHAR that holds prefixed strings for manual matches (`manual_${ts}_…`) and replay-fallback matches (`replay_${ts}`); those sort lexicographically *above* normal numeric IDs and would have given the wrong \"most recent\" persona row in some cases. Caught in code review before shipping.) Sign-in now completes end-to-end on prod.\n\n**2. Tier Ladder Preview no longer shows duplicate ranks.** The admin-panel preview was synthesising sample MMR values and bucketing them through `TierBadge`, which legitimately collapsed adjacent samples into the same band — producing the \"Paladin / Paladin / Templar / Knight / Knight / Footman / Footman …\" row the user spotted. Rewritten to iterate the canonical `MMR_TIERS` array directly: one row per defined tier, showing each tier's actual MMR floor (and \"#1 leaderboard only\" for King). What you see in the preview is now exactly what the leaderboard renders, with no synthetic samples in between.\n\n**3. V1 ladder code removed (full edition is V3-only).** `MMR_TIERS_V1`, the `useV3` prop on `TierBadge`, the `data.useV3` field on the leaderboard, and the dual-column V1/V3 admin preview all deleted. Single canonical `MMR_TIERS` export drives everything. (The deeper `use_v3_trueskill` *rating-engine* toggle in `src/db/index.js` is intentionally untouched — that switches the actual MMR maths, not the display ladder.)\n\n**4. Real heraldic badge artwork on the leaderboard's rank legend.** The small \"worst → … → best\" strip above the leaderboard table was rendering the placeholder emojis (🌾 Peasant 🥾 Vagabond 🏴 Outlaw 📜 Apprentice 🐎 Squire 🗡️ Footman 🛡️ Knight ⚔️ Templar ✨ Paladin 🪓 Warlord 👑 King). Now uses the actual `/badges/tier-N-name.png` artwork for each chip — same heraldic shields used inside the leaderboard rows themselves — and the King chip therefore renders the actual gold crown badge. Emoji is kept as the `onError` fallback so a missing PNG never breaks the strip.\n\n**5. Draft Assistant + Draft Stats merged into one nav item.** They were two separate dropdown buttons under Tools that rendered nearly identical content (the `/draft` page has always had an Assistant tab built in). Removed the duplicate \"Draft Assistant\" dropdown item; the surviving entry is renamed to \"Draft & Assistant\" and routes to `/draft`, which already exposes both the assistant and the stats. The standalone `/draft-assistant` and `/draft-stats` URL routes are kept so any bookmarks still resolve.\n\nFooter version bump v5.82 → v5.83.",
    "author": "System"
  },
  {
    "version": "5.82",
    "title": "Heraldic rank names on every player badge, King reserved for #1, MMR ladder spread, Steam-auth diagnostics",
    "published_at": "2026-05-06",
    "content": "Four reported issues in one batch.\n\n**1. Player badges now show the heraldic NAME, not \"Tier VII\".** The 8-tier MMR badge that sits next to a player's name on `/player/:id` and on the home dashboard previously rendered the placeholder labels \"Tier I\" through \"Tier VIII\". They now use the same heraldic catalog as the leaderboard ladder — Apprentice → Squire → Footman → Knight → Templar → Paladin → Warlord → King — so a single player's badge reads, e.g., **\"🪓 Warlord 7154\"** instead of **\"☀️ Tier VII 7154\"**. One source of truth across `MmrBadge` and the leaderboard's `TierBadge`.\n\n**2. King is reserved for the #1 leaderboard player.** Until now King was just \"MMR ≥ 6500\" under the V3 ladder, which meant the entire active inhouse pool landed in the same top tier. King is now a `leaderOnly` rank: every other badge maxes out at **Warlord** regardless of how high the MMR climbs, and the badge promotes to King only when the player is the current #1 on the all-time leaderboard. `PlayerProfile.jsx` and the home dashboard `HomeBanner` each fetch `/api/leaderboard?limit=1` and pass `isLeader` down to `MmrBadge`; the leaderboard page passes `isLeader={i === 0}` to `TierBadge` for the top row. `getTier()` and `decodeMmrBadge()` both skip `leaderOnly` tiers when no leader flag is set.\n\n**3. MMR thresholds spread out so not everyone is the same tier.** With a 5000-MMR start point and a typical active player sitting at 6000–7500, the V3 ladder squeezed eight bands into a 1500-MMR window and dumped almost everyone into King/Warlord. Recalibrated:\n\n- King — leaderboard #1 only (no MMR threshold)\n- Warlord — 7000+\n- Paladin — 6500+\n- Templar — 6200+\n- Knight — 5900+\n- Footman — 5600+\n- Squire — 5300+\n- Apprentice — 5000+ (default starting band)\n- Outlaw — 4500+\n- Vagabond — 4000+\n- Peasant — < 4000\n\nSame heraldic names apply to the `MmrBadge` next to player names so both ladders stay in sync.\n\n**4. Steam sign-in `?auth=error` — granular diagnostics.** v5.80 fixed `?auth=invalid` (the verify body was being re-encoded by Express's qs parser, breaking Steam OpenID's signature check). The remaining `?auth=error` reports come from the **catch block** that fires after verify succeeds — meaning DB lookup, BigInt parse, or session save is throwing. The catch block was previously a black hole: it logged `'[Steam Auth] Error:'` and the full err object, which on some PM2 / Node combos prints as `[object Object]` with no useful context. The handler now logs the error name, message, code, and a 6-line stack on three separate lines so the very next PM2 log entry will tell us exactly which post-verify step failed in production. Once `cd ~/Dota-Stats-Full && bash deploy.sh` rolls this out, attempt sign-in then check `pm2 logs oi-bot --lines 80` — the failing step will be obvious.\n\nFooter bump v5.81 → v5.82.",
    "author": "System"
  },
  {
    "version": "5.81",
    "title": "Public profile graduates the v5.74 mockup — eight customization knobs are now real",
    "published_at": "2026-05-06",
    "content": "**Closing a long-standing gap between `/admin/profile-sandbox` and the public `/player/:id` page.** Until now, the sandbox previewed a much richer profile than what visitors actually saw on real player pages. Eight features lived in the mockup only — they couldn't be saved, validated, or rendered for real visitors. This release graduates all eight from sandbox to live profile.\n\n**What's new on the public profile.** When a player saves any of these in `/settings/profile`, they now appear on the page everyone sees when clicking that player from the leaderboard:\n\n- **Pinned achievement** — pick any earned (non-secret) achievement to display next to your pinned hero / pinned match.\n- **Custom flair override** (Pro) — replace the auto-derived flair (e.g. \"Hard Carry\", \"Mid Threat\") with one of 8 free or 8 Pro-tier flairs (\"GOAT\", \"Mid Lord\", \"Hook Wizard\", etc).\n- **Pinned-hero border colour** — 7 preset accent colours (brass, amber, radiant, dire, royal purple, steel blue, parchment) painted around your pinned hero portrait.\n- **Most-played heroes strip** (toggle) — auto-rendered top-5 heroes with games and win rate, pulled from your existing hero stats.\n- **Win/loss streak badge** (toggle) — 🔥 W3+ or ❄️ L3+ chip auto-shown when you're on a meaningful run.\n- **Animated frame shimmer** (Pro) — a subtle pulsing glow on your profile frame.\n- **Heraldic background pattern** (Pro) — a tasteful 45° hatched accent fill behind the customization block.\n- **Social chips** — Twitch / YouTube / Steam profile links rendered as branded pills. Server-side allowlist enforces https + official domains so you can't sneak in arbitrary URLs.\n\n**Schema.** All 11 fields live in a single new `extras` JSONB column on `player_profiles` — no per-knob migrations needed when we add more. Validated server-side via the new `validateExtras()` in `src/profileCosmetics.js`; Pro-only knobs (animated frame, heraldic bg, custom flair, premium flair values) are gated in the `POST /api/me/profile` handler. Existing rows with no extras render exactly as before — defaults are: top-heroes ON, streak ON, everything else OFF / null.\n\n**Settings UI.** A new \"Profile extras\" section at the bottom of `/settings/profile` exposes every knob: a 4-toggle grid for the booleans, a swatch picker for the hero border, an `<select>` populated from your real earned achievements (calls `/api/players/:id/achievements`), a Pro-gated flair override with optgroup-separated free vs premium choices, and three URL fields for the social chips.\n\n**Public render.** All new fields slot into the existing customization block on `PlayerProfile.jsx` between the page header and the AI Scouting Report — no layout reshuffle of pages without customizations.\n\n**Mirrored to the community edition** identically (same column, same validator, same UI). Footer bump v5.80 → v5.81.",
    "author": "System"
  }
